Title:
Local AI for Cyber Security (Benchmarking local vs cloud AI for vuln discovery)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
The post benchmarks four approaches (Semgrep, a cloud “agentic” pentest workflow, cloud SOTA models with a code-review skill, and a local model with a custom harness) against a known authenticated LFI in phpIPAM’s API. The author shows that model choice mattered less than the review harness: a file-by-file local review loop consistently found the bug, while Semgrep missed it and cloud agentic workflows were expensive and inconsistent. The benchmark vuln is an authenticated LFI/path traversal via an unsanitized `controller` parameter concatenated into `require_once` (CVE-2026-12194), affecting phpIPAM instances with the API enabled. The same local-harness approach then found a real authenticated RCE in myVesta via command injection into `exec` in an FTP username deletion path (CVE-2026-12195), which was patched quickly. It highlights practical constraints: token burn, false positives, and weak threat-model/context understanding for nuanced access control issues. Useful for red teamers, pentesters, and security engineers evaluating AI-assisted code review workflows under cost/privacy constraints.

Technical Focus:
- Authenticated Local File Inclusion (LFI) via PHP `require_once` path construction
- Agentic AI pentesting vs skill-based prompting vs deterministic harnessing
- Local LLM code review at scale (per-file context windows, report aggregation)
- Token/cost benchmarking and privacy tradeoffs (cloud vs local inference)
- Command injection leading to authenticated RCE (`exec` misuse) in PHP apps
- Limitations: false positives and missing higher-level authorization/threat-model issues

Use Cases:
- Building a local, privacy-preserving AI-assisted SAST/code review pipeline
- Triage and discovery of web app vulns (LFI/RFI patterns, command injection) across large repos
- Comparing ROI of Semgrep/rule-based SAST vs LLM-based review workflows
- Augmenting penetration tests with automated per-file review and structured reporting
- Rapid vulnerability research and responsible disclosure support (CVE tracking, patch validation)

Keywords:
phpIPAM, myVesta, PHP, Local File Inclusion, LFI, path traversal, require_once, authenticated vulnerability, API token, Semgrep, SAST, agentic AI, Strix, Claude Code, Cursor, GPT-5.5, Qwen 3.6 27B, large context window, token consumption, command injection, exec, RCE, CVE-2026-12194, CVE-2026-12195